1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,620
For VOA Learning English, this is Everyday Grammar.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:13,520
This week, we are going to show you how to give advice using modal verbs.

3
00:00:15,020 --> 00:00:23,720
Modal verbs, called modals for short, are auxiliary verbs that express a speaker's attitude

4
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:27,160
and the strength of that attitude.

5
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:31,400
For example, he should visit Prague.

6
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:38,960
In this sentence, should is the modal verb and visit is the main verb.

7
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:44,380
The simple form of a verb goes after a modal.

8
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:50,740
Do not add the third person s to a verb after a modal.

9
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:56,020
It would sound strange to say, he should visit Prague.

10
00:00:56,610 --> 00:00:59,880
Or, he should to visit Prague.

11
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:04,540
The correct way is, he should visit Prague.

12
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:08,960
There are about 17 modals in English.

13
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,220
Grammar experts do not agree on an exact number.

14
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:21,040
Today, we will focus on three common modals used for giving advice.

15
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:25,740
Should, ought to, and had better.

16
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:29,080
Let's start with should.

17
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:32,280
Should has multiple meanings.

18
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:41,060
It can be used to express certainty, such as, he should be here by five o'clock.

19
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:46,840
Should can be a substitute for the conditional word if.

20
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:53,980
You might hear someone say, should you need help, just ask me.

21
00:01:55,340 --> 00:02:02,900
But more often, we use should to give suggestions and friendly advice, such as,

22
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,260
You should apply for that job.

23
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:09,160
Or, You should try that new restaurant.

24
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:16,860
The past form of the modal should is should have plus the past participle.

25
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,220
For example, I should have brought my wallet.

26
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:26,940
Notice that the main verb brought is in the past participle form.

27
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:34,160
Use should have to express regret or a negative feeling about the past.

28
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:39,800
Imagine you trusted someone, and that person later cheated on you.

29
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:44,720
You could say, I should have known better than to trust him.

30
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:49,360
The Beatles used the expression in a popular song.

31
00:03:04,870 --> 00:03:08,610
The next modal we will talk about is ought to.

32
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:12,990
Ought to is another modal for giving advice.

33
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:21,310
Sometimes, ought to sounds more like oughta, as in this romantic song by Al Green.

34
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:00,360
Ought to is similar in meaning to should, but it is not used as often.

35
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:10,520
In modern American English, ought to is seldom used with the past tense or in the question form.

36
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:14,160
Let's move on to had better.

37
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:19,340
Had better is stronger than should and ought to.

38
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:23,340
Had better carries an indirect threat.

39
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:29,380
For example, if you said, You had better finish that report.

40
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:33,020
You are not making a polite suggestion.

41
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,240
You are making an indirect threat.

42
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,200
In other words, if you don't finish the report, you are in trouble.

43
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:49,800
Authority figures sometimes use had better when speaking to people below them.

44
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:54,100
Parents also use this form often.

45
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,400
Listen to cartoon character Mallory Archer.

46
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:03,140
Mallory is the head of a spy agency.

47
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:07,440
People think she is arrogant and heartless.

48
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:12,160
Listen to her tone when she uses had better.

49
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:15,720
Oh, I'll send up some help.

50
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:18,440
And Missy, you had better watch it.

51
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:24,120
As you can hear, Mallory is not making a polite suggestion.

52
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:28,060
She is threatening someone in a lower position.

53
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:32,280
Had better is not always impolite.

54
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:38,520
It could express a sense of urgency, as in, Your plane is leaving.

55
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:40,140
You had better run.

56
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:45,300
In other words, If you don't run, you will miss your flight.

57
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:50,500
Had better has no past tense or question form.

58
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:58,400
Should, ought to, and had better can be difficult for English learners to hear.

59
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:04,320
Native speakers often shorten these words in casual conversation.

60
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:14,820
In rapid speech, modals seem to disappear because they are shortened and often fall on unstressed syllables.

61
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:18,260
We will read some examples for you.

62
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:22,560
The first sentence will be in slow, careful speech.

63
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,600
Then, we will read it again in rapid, informal speech.

64
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:32,940
I should have been listening to what she had to say.

65
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,140
I should have been listening to what she had to say.

66
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:40,280
You ought to be ashamed of yourself.

67
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:42,600
You ought to be ashamed of yourself.

68
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:46,940
You had better decide what you want to do.

69
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:49,300
You'd better decide what you want to do.

70
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,380
That's all the advice we have for you today.

71
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:55,740
There is much more to learn about, modals.

72
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:59,600
We will cover them in more detail in future episodes.

73
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:05,440
Until then, you should practice modals with British punk band The Clash.

74
00:07:34,170 --> 00:07:35,510
I'm Pete Musto.

75
00:07:36,470 --> 00:07:37,510
And I'm Jill Robbins.

76
00:07:38,730 --> 00:07:39,650
Now it's your turn.

77
00:07:40,930 --> 00:07:44,190
Think of a friend that you would like to give some advice.

78
00:07:45,850 --> 00:07:47,590
Write to us in the comments section.

79
00:07:48,290 --> 00:07:54,310
Write two sentences using should, ought to, or had better, giving advice.

80
00:07:55,210 --> 00:07:56,130
We'll give you feedback.

